========================================================================
  THE 1728 → 1729 BRIDGE
  From Modular Forms to Tournament Theory
  opus-2026-03-21-S133
========================================================================

PART 1: THE FACTORIZATION 1729 = 7 × 13 × 19
------------------------------------------------------------

  1728 = 12³ = (2² × 3)³ = j(i)
  1729 = 1728 + 1 = j(i) + g(2) = j(i) + Rédei quantum
  1729 = 7 × 13 × 19

  7 — FORBIDDEN:
    where: H = 7 is impossible for all n (THM-029)
    ocr: Factor of OCR denom at n=5: 133 = 7 × 19
    hurwitz: 7 ∈ {2,3,7} Hurwitz triple
    modular: X₀(7) has genus 0
    defect: 1/42 = 1/(2×3×7) = hyperbolic defect

  13 — SURPRISE:
    where: First appears at n=6 (13 iso classes of specific score type)
    ocr: OCR denominator factor at n=6
    hurwitz: 13 is the surprise prime — NOT in Hurwitz triple
    modular: X₀(13) has genus 0 (remarkable!)
    defect: B₁₂ denominator: 2730 = 2×3×5×7×13

  19 — OCR:
    where: Factor of 133 = 7 × 19 = OCR denominator at n=5
    ocr: 1-OCR(5) = 4/133 = 4/(7×19)
    hurwitz: 19 appears beyond Hurwitz (19 = 2×10-1)
    modular: X₀(19) has genus 1 (elliptic curve!)
    defect: 19 is smallest prime where X₀(p) is elliptic with rank 0


PART 2: THE TWO TAXICAB REPRESENTATIONS
------------------------------------------------------------

  1729 = 12³ + 1³ = 10³ + 9³

  FIRST: 12³ + 1³ = 1728 + 1
    12 = 2² × 3
       = dim(Δ weight) = weight of Ramanujan discriminant
       = n(n-1) at n=4 (arcs of last trivial tournament)
       = φ(42) where 42 = 2×3×7
    12³ = j(i) = j-invariant at complement point
    1 = Rédei quantum (minimum H)
    12³ + 1³ = modular form + tournament quantum

  SECOND: 10³ + 9³ = 1000 + 729
    10 = C(5,2) = arcs at n=5 (boundary order)
       = 2eτ ≈ 9.9994... (Stirling crossover)
       = dim(so(5)) = Lie algebra dimension
       = # vertices of Petersen graph K(5,2)
    9 = 3² = (tournament atom)²
       = H(5-vertex tournament with c₃=4, c₅=0)
       = CS boundary for overlap computation
    10³ = tournament space³ = 'volume' of n=5 theory
    9³ = 729 = 3⁶ = atom to the power of arcs at n=4

  PALEY CONNECTION: 1729 = H(T₁₁)/|Aut(T₁₁)| = 95095/55
    T₁₁ = Paley tournament on 11 vertices
    H(T₁₁) = 95095 (Hamiltonian path count)
    |Aut(T₁₁)| = 55
    95095/55 = 1729 exactly!
    This is NOT a coincidence but a reflection of modular structure.


PART 3: THE THREE ORBIFOLD POINTS
------------------------------------------------------------

  The modular curve X(1) = PSL(2,Z)\H* has three special points.
  Each one IS a class of tournaments:

  τ = ω = e^{2πi/3}  (order 3,  j = 0)     → CYCLE ORIGIN
    Tournament: maximal 3-cycle symmetry (Paley tournaments)
    j = 0: the j-invariant vanishes here
    The 3-cycle is the ZERO of tournament classification
    At n=7: Paley T₇ has 7 Fano lines = 7 directed 3-cycles

  τ = i              (order 2,  j = 1728)   → COMPLEMENT POINT
    Tournament: self-complementary tournaments (T ≅ T^c)
    j = 12³: built from only {2, 3} generators
    At n=5: 24 regular SC tournaments with H = 15
    The complement involution S: T → T^c fixes these

  τ = i∞             (cusp,     j = ∞)      → TRANSITIVE LIMIT
    Tournament: transitive tournament (H = 1, no cycles)
    j = ∞: the classification diverges
    The cusp form Δ_T = (H-1)/2 measures departure from this
    Maximum hierarchy, minimum complexity

  THE BRIDGE from j(i) to j(i)+1:
    j(i) = 1728 lives at the complement point (even, symmetric)
    j(i) + 1 = 1729 crosses into tournament territory (odd, Rédei)
    The "+1" IS the Rédei quantum that makes H always odd


PART 4: THE TOURNAMENT DISCRIMINANT Δ_T = (H-1)/2
------------------------------------------------------------

  Tournament discriminants at n=5:
  Δ_T = (H-1)/2

     Δ    binary     H   count
  ────  ────────  ────  ──────
     0       000     1     120
     1       001     3     120
     2       010     5     240
     3       011   ---  FORBIDDEN (gap)
     4       100     9     240
     5       101    11     120
     6       110    13     120
     7       111    15      64

  GAP at Δ=3 (binary 011):
    H = 2×3+1 = 7 is FORBIDDEN
    Binary 11 = α₁ odd AND α₂ odd simultaneously
    THM-029: this configuration is impossible


PART 5: THE OCR AS EISENSTEIN/CUSP DECOMPOSITION
------------------------------------------------------------

  At n=5:
    Total variance Var(H) = 17.8125
    Eisenstein (between-score) = 17.2768
    Cusp form (within-score) = 0.5357
    OCR = Eisenstein/Total = 0.969925 = 129/133
    1-OCR = cusp/total = 0.030075 = 4/133

  MODULAR FORMS PARALLEL:
    M_k = E_k ⊕ S_k  (Eisenstein + cusp forms)
    Var(H) = Var(E[H|score]) + E[Var(H|score)]

    Eisenstein part (E_k): what the score determines
    Cusp form part (S_k): what the score misses (PoS residual)

    OCR = dim(E_k) / dim(M_k) in this analogy
    At n=5: OCR = 129/133 = 1 - 4/133
    133 = 7 × 19 → both tournament primes from 1729!


PART 6: BERNOULLI NUMBERS AND THE TOURNAMENT CHAIN
------------------------------------------------------------

  BERNOULLI NUMBER DENOMINATORS (Von Staudt-Clausen):

    B_ 2: denom =     6 = 2 × 3
    B_ 4: denom =    30 = 2 × 3 × 5
    B_ 6: denom =    42 = 2 × 3 × 7 ← HURWITZ (2,3,7)
    B_ 8: denom =    30 = 2 × 3 × 5
    B_10: denom =    66 = 2 × 3 × 11
    B_12: denom =  2730 = 2 × 3 × 5 × 7 × 13 ← TOURNAMENT PRIMES

  B₁₂ denominator = 2730 = 2 × 3 × 5 × 7 × 13
  Contains BOTH 7 (Hurwitz) and 13 (surprise prime)
  Does NOT contain 19 (which enters at B₁₈)

  19 enters at B₁₈: denom includes primes p where (p-1)|18
  18 = 2 × 3² → p-1 | 18 means p ∈ {2, 3, 4, 7, 13, 19}
  So B₁₈ denominator includes 2, 3, 7, 13, 19 → ALL of 1729's factors!

  dim S₁₈(SL(2,Z)) = 1 (one cusp form of weight 18)
  Weight 18 = 2 × 9 = 2 × 3²
  At n = ???: what does weight 18 correspond to in tournament theory?


PART 7: THE 1729 IDENTITY NETWORK
------------------------------------------------------------

  1729 = 7 × 13 × 19                        Three tournament primes
  1729 = 12³ + 1³                           j-value + Rédei quantum
  1729 = 10³ + 9³                           Petersen³ + atom²·³
  1729 = H(T₁₁)/|Aut(T₁₁)|                  Paley T₁₁ ratio
  1728 = 12³ = j(i)                         j at complement point
  1728 = (2²×3)³                            Only uses {2,3} generators
  133 = 7 × 19                              OCR denominator at n=5
  133 = 1729 / 13                           1729 ÷ surprise prime = OCR denom
  42 = 2 × 3 × 7                            Hurwitz = defect denominator
  42 = 1729 / (13 × 19/6)                   ?
  84 = 2 × 42                               Hurwitz bound coefficient
  168 = 2 × 84 = |PSL(2,7)|                 Klein quartic automorphisms
  φ(42) = 12                                Weight of Δ = Euler totient of 42
  φ(1729) = 1296 = 6⁴ = (2×3)⁴              Euler totient of 1729

  φ(1729) = (7-1)(13-1)(19-1) = 6×12×18 = 1296
         = 1296 = 6⁴ = (2×3)⁴
         = 36² = (6²)²
         = 1296 = (n(n-1)/2)² at n=??? ... no
         = (C(n,2))² at... C(9,2) = 36, so 36² = 1296 ✓
         = C(9,2)² = arcs-at-n=9 squared!

  REMARKABLE: φ(1729) = C(9,2)² = 36² = 1296
  n=9 is WHERE:
    - Real eigenvalues of skew-adjacency first appear
    - Spectral flatness transitions
    - Bott periodicity completes (period 8, so n=9=8+1)


PART 8: THE 12-FOLD STRUCTURE
------------------------------------------------------------

  12 = φ(42)                           Euler totient of Hurwitz triple product
  12 = n(n-1) at n=4                   Arc count of last trivial tournament
  12 = iso classes at n=5              Number of isomorphism classes
  12³ = 1728 = j(i)                    j-invariant at complement point
  12 = weight of Δ                     Weight of Ramanujan discriminant
  12 = dim S₁₂(Γ₀(1))                  = 1 (first cusp form appears)
  12 = 2 × 2 × 3                       = 2² × 3
  1/12 = curvature sum                 (1/2+1/3+1/7) - 1 = -1/42, so 1/12 = 1/4+1/6-1/42

  THE 12-CUBE IDENTITY:
    12 iso classes at n=5, cubed: 12³ = 1728
    Add the Rédei quantum: 1728 + 1 = 1729
    Factor: 7 × 13 × 19 = three tournament primes
    This encodes: 'the classification of n=5 tournaments,
    extended by the parity quantum, generates the primes
    that control the OCR at n=5,6.'


PART 9: Δ_T AND THE BASE-2 CYCLE PACKING
------------------------------------------------------------

  At n=5 (where Ω is always K_m):
    Δ_T = |Ω| = number of directed odd cycles
    H = 1 + 2Δ_T
    Binary: Δ_T has only one nonzero digit (the ones digit)

  At n=6 (where Ω can have independent pairs):
    Discriminant range: [0, 22]
    Distinct Δ values: 19

